    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text supplied; no apparent truncation or garbling.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports test-set prediction performance across batch sizes, a different task and measure from the claim’s classification generalization gap.",
    "gate_note": "The paper studies permeability regression on synthetic porous media, not fitting random labels or train–test error gaps. It reports separate 3D batch-size sweeps for PointNet and 3D-CNN; it does not state that epochs were held fixed.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures permeability regression performance across batch sizes, whereas V5 concerns image classifiers and language models under fixed epoch budgets. Epoch counts and stopping rules for the batch-size sweeps are not reported. Its architecture and feature-size comparisons also do not test any listed version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that representing pore boundaries as point clouds reduces GPU memory requirements, permits exploration of larger batches, and enables accurate, fast permeability prediction. Performance declines on statistically different synthetic rocks and natural samples. They do not establish a fixed-epoch large-batch generalization result.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Regression on synthetic porous-media data; PointNet and a matched CNN comparison; batch-size sweep, with epoch budget not stated.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported test-set R2 varies with batch size for both networks; the best reported scores occur at batch size 1024 for the point-cloud network and 512 for 3D-CNN, whose larger batch runs were prevented by GPU memory limits.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "For permeability regression, PointNet test R2 rises from 0.76297 at batch 8 to 0.99151 at batch 1024; 3D-CNN rises from 0.73434 at batch 8 to 0.97599 at batch 512. Intermediate results are not monotonic. CNN batches 1024 and 2048 could not run because of GPU memory limits.",
        "proposed_claim": "Batch size affects held-out permeability regression performance, with larger tested batches generally performing better in these experiments; the training-budget dependence is unknown.",
